\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Probing Neural Representations.}
Linear probes are a standard tool for investigating learned representations~\citep{alain2017understanding,belinkov2017morphology,conneau2018probing,belinkov2019analysis}. \citet{hewitt2019structural} show that syntax trees are linearly recoverable from contextual embeddings. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks and selectivity to distinguish genuine signal from probe memorization. We extend probing from linguistic to safety-relevant properties, adopting the control task methodology to validate our probes.

\paragraph{Causal Interpretability.}
Causal abstraction~\citep{geiger2021causal}, causal mediation analysis~\citep{vig2020causal}, ROME~\citep{meng2022locating}, and ACDC~\citep{conmy2023circuit} establish causal relationships between representations and model behavior. These methods require expensive interventions during inference. Our approach is explicitly correlational: we establish that safety commitment is \textit{decodable} from hidden states without claiming causality, trading causal guarantees for practical applicability.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance on hidden-state trajectories, SafeSwitch~\citep{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers for safety interventions, and SIREN~\citep{siren} monitors internal representations during generation. Representation Engineering~\citep{zou2023representation} identifies linear safety directions, and \citet{arditi2024refusal} show refusal is mediated by a single direction. These works identify static safety directions; we study the \textit{temporal evolution} of safety commitment during multi-step reasoning. On the attack side, GCG~\citep{zou2023gcg} generates universal adversarial suffixes, while SmoothLLM~\citep{robey2023smoothllm} and perplexity filtering~\citep{alon2023perplexity} defend at the input level. We focus on detecting self-jailbreak within the model's own reasoning, without adversarial input modification. We reproduce TrajGuard on reasoning models (\cref{sec:negative}) and find it fails due to gradual rather than abrupt transitions.

\paragraph{Reasoning Model Safety.}
DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1} and QwQ~\citep{qwq} produce extended CoT that creates new safety challenges. \citet{harmthoughts} document self-jailbreak through gradual rationalization; \citet{selfjailbreak} show longer chains increase circumvention probability. SafeRemind~\citep{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered safety reminders; SafeThink~\citep{safethink} steers early reasoning steps. \citet{chen2025unfaithful} reveal that CoT can be unfaithful to actual decisions, undermining text-based monitoring. \citet{wang2025lrmsafety} survey safety challenges across the reasoning pipeline. We address a complementary question: given that commitment occurs somewhere in the chain, what \textit{modality} best detects it?

\paragraph{Process Supervision and Sequential Monitoring.}
Process Reward Models~\citep{uesato2022process,lightman2023prm,wang2024prm} evaluate reasoning correctness at the step level. We instead assess reasoning \textit{safety}. Our temporal detection problem also connects to change-point detection: BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts. We apply it as a baseline (\cref{sec:negative}) but find that safety commitment is gradual rather than abrupt, motivating temporal probes that capture gradual transitions.

\paragraph{Cross-Model Representation Comparison.}
CKA~\citep{kornblith2019cka} measures representational similarity; model stitching~\citep{bansal2021stitching} tests functional compatibility through linear maps. Both assume shared structure enables alignment. Our negative transfer results (\cref{sec:transfer}) show that safety commitment is encoded in model-specific geometric configurations rather than a universal subspace.

\section{Problem Setup and Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Safety Commitment Point}
\label{sec:commitment-point}

When a reasoning model is jailbroken, its CoT transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation. We define the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP) as the first step after which safety reasoning permanently ceases. We operationalize this on HarmThoughts~\cite{harmthoughts} traces: the CP is the earliest step $t^*$ such that no subsequent step contains safety reasoning. Steps before CP are \emph{pre-commitment}; steps after are \emph{post-commitment}.

\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

Let $s_t$ denote the probe score at step $t$, $\tau$ the threshold, and $t^*$ the annotated CP.

\textbf{Coverage}: fraction of jailbreak traces where any step exceeds $\tau$. \textbf{Pre-CP FPR}: we report both step-level (pooling all pre-CP steps) and trace-level (mean per-trace FPR, treating each trace as an independent observation for paired statistical comparison). \textbf{Precision}: fraction of threshold-exceeding steps that are post-CP. \textbf{Lead time}: steps between first detection and CP ($t^* - t_{\text{detect}}$).

All modality comparisons use a two-sided paired permutation test (1000 resamples) on trace-level values. We conduct 12 hypothesis tests (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models) and apply the Holm-Bonferroni step-down procedure ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Tests are ranked by $p$-value: R1-8B precision ($p{<}0.001$, threshold 0.00417, significant), OT-7B balanced accuracy ($p{<}0.001$, threshold 0.00455, significant), OT-7B precision ($p{<}0.001$, threshold 0.005, significant), OT-7B FPR ($p{<}0.001$, threshold 0.00556, significant), R1-8B accuracy ($p{=}0.005$, threshold 0.00625, significant), R1-8B FPR ($p{=}0.044$, threshold 0.00714, not significant). Five of twelve tests survive correction, contributed by two independent models at 7-8B scale. This procedure treats all metrics as independent; in practice, metrics exhibit substantial correlation (Precision-FPR $|r|{=}0.42$--$0.69$ across models; Accuracy-Precision $|r|{=}0.37$--$0.55$), so the effective number of independent tests is fewer than 12. We use detection criterion $K{=}1$ (single crossing) throughout.

\subsection{Models and Data}
\label{sec:models-data}

We study four models spanning two architectures and two scales:

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{Traces} & \textbf{Test} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209 & 43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228 & 47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100 & 20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95 & 19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Models studied. All use HarmThoughts traces with step-level CP annotations. Train/val/test split follows 60/20/20 ratio. Total: 632 traces across four models.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B~\cite{openthinker}) is trained with STILL~\cite{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data comes from HarmThoughts~\cite{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations. The two 7-8B models provide stronger statistical power ($n{=}43$--$47$) than the 32B test sets ($n{=}19$--$20$), partially explaining why significant results concentrate at the smaller scale.

\subsection{Detection Methods}
\label{sec:detection-methods}

\paragraph{Hidden-state (HS) probes.} At each step, we extract the last-token hidden state from a selected layer and train L2-regularized logistic regression (balanced class weights) on pre-CP vs.\ post-CP labels. Layer selection uses the product of balanced accuracy and crossing rate on the validation set; best layers: R1-8B layer 14 (44\% depth), OT-7B layer 16 (57\% depth), R1-32B layer 10 (16\%), QwQ-32B layer 2 (3\%).

\paragraph{Text probes.} We encode each step's text with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384-d sentence embeddings) and apply the same logistic regression procedure. This represents detection performance from surface-level signals alone.

\paragraph{Temporal windowing.} For window size $W$, we concatenate features from steps $[t{-}W{+}1, \ldots, t]$ into a single input. We evaluate $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$, selecting the operating-point $W$ per-model on the validation set.

\paragraph{GRU sequence models.} We train GRU classifiers on full step sequences in single-layer (best layer) and multi-layer (7 layers concatenated) variants. Details in \cref{app:gru_details}.

\paragraph{Training procedure.} We train with Adam (learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, default $\beta_1{=}0.9$, $\beta_2{=}0.999$), batch size 16, maximum 50 epochs with early stopping (patience 10). Inputs are z-score normalized per dimension.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\textbf{BOCPD}~\citep{adams2007bayesian}: applied to probe score sequences to test whether commitment produces an abrupt distributional shift. \textbf{CUSUM}: parametric sequential detection accumulating evidence for a mean shift, with configurations $(\delta, h)$ swept in \cref{app:bocpd_config}. \textbf{Mahalanobis distance}: following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}, computes geometric separation between pre- and post-CP hidden states. \textbf{Position-only baseline}: predicts ``unsafe'' based solely on step index, testing whether probes capture signal beyond positional correlations.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate contrastive trace probes across four reasoning models (R1-8B, OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ-32B), two signal modalities (hidden states and text), and multiple temporal aggregation strategies. Our experiments address four questions: (1) How much does temporal context improve detection? (2) Does the choice of signal modality matter? (3) Can sequence models detect unsafe reasoning earlier? (4) Do probes transfer across models?

\subsection{Temporal Context as Primary Factor}
\label{sec:temporal_primary}

The most consistent finding across all experimental conditions is that temporal context improves probe precision and reduces FPR across all four models and both modalities. For HS probes, all four models show improving precision and FPR with larger windows; OT-7B balanced accuracy is the exception, peaking at $W{=}3$ and declining at larger windows while its precision and FPR continue to improve. \cref{fig:window_ablation} shows the pattern for R1-8B.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Effect of temporal window size on R1-8B HS probe performance. Precision increases monotonically from 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR decreases from 9.65\% to 4.83\%. Coverage decreases as larger windows make the probe more conservative.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

On R1-8B, increasing window size from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$ improves balanced accuracy from 77.8\% to 81.0\% (+3.2pp), precision from 69.3\% to 82.2\% (+12.9pp), and reduces step-level FPR from 9.65\% to 4.83\% ($-$4.8pp). QwQ-32B shows a similar monotonic pattern (+3.2pp accuracy, +7.4pp precision, $-$5.9pp FPR from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$). R1-32B exhibits a non-monotonic trajectory: accuracy peaks at $W{=}10$ then declines below the $W{=}1$ baseline by $W{=}25$ (71.8\% $\rightarrow$ 70.3\%), though precision and FPR still improve (+1.7pp, $-$5.5pp). We hypothesize that this decline reflects overfitting to the limited training set ($n{=}60$) as feature dimensionality grows with $W$, though alternative explanations (e.g., genuine distributional differences in R1-32B trace structure) cannot be ruled out. OT-7B shows a split temporal pattern: HS balanced accuracy peaks at $W{=}3$ (68.8\%) and declines with larger windows, but precision and FPR continue to improve (+6.1pp precision and $-$4.0pp FPR from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$). Text probes improve normally (+3.3pp accuracy, $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; \cref{app:ot7b_details}). Across all four models, HS FPR consistently decreases with larger windows (\cref{tab:temporal_main}). Larger windows make probes more conservative: fewer traces cross the detection threshold (coverage decreases from 72.1\% to 48.8\% on R1-8B under $K{=}1$ criterion), but those that do are classified with higher confidence.

The coverage-quality trade-off depends on the detection criterion. Under $K{=}1$ (single threshold crossing), larger $W$ reduces coverage because the smoothed probe score crosses the boundary fewer times. Under stricter criteria ($K{=}5$, requiring consecutive crossings), larger $W$ can improve effective coverage by eliminating transient false crossings. We report $K{=}1$ results throughout for interpretability, noting that deployment systems would tune $K$ alongside $W$.

The key observation is that temporal improvement in precision (+1.7 to +12.9pp) and FPR ($-$4.0 to $-$5.9pp) is consistently positive across all four models and both modalities. For text probes, temporal context is universally beneficial. For HS probes, all four models show improving precision and FPR, though OT-7B balanced accuracy saturates at $W{=}3$. Observing multiple reasoning steps provides the dominant source of discriminative information for FPR reduction.

